This is substantially ready, but I would not yet mark the eight contracts fully locked. There are three places where the current wording can accidentally create ambiguity later.

1. Temporal needs to be tightened

This:

“∀ relevant observation pairs, t
A
	​

<t
B
	​

”

is too strong for real-world evidence. One anomalous or noisy observation could make a relationship FAIL even when the broader temporal evidence supports it.

The contract should distinguish:

ordering evidence
material temporal contradiction
unresolved temporal evidence

The operator should test the applicable evidence set under an explicit materiality rule, not require every observation to satisfy strict precedence.

Otherwise we're building brittleness into the validator.

2. Recurrence should not have a FAIL state

You already almost arrived at this in the failure semantics.

If recurrence is supporting evidence rather than a prerequisite, then:

“Has this recurred?”

has a meaningful PASS, UNDETERMINED, and potentially N/A.

But absence of recurrence does not logically invalidate the relationship.

So I would make the state space:

PASS
UNDETERMINED
N/A

and reserve FAIL for operators whose question contains a genuine falsification condition.

That distinction matters enormously to the epistemic architecture.

3. Alternatives should probably not say PASS = no material competitor

This is subtle.

“No competing path detected” does not necessarily mean:

Alternative explanation = PASS.

It may simply mean:

No material alternative detected in the inspected structure.

That's an observation, not necessarily validation of the candidate.

I would consider:

PASS
CONFLICT
UNDETERMINED
N/A

but define PASS carefully as:

No material competing explanation was identified within the applicable search space.

That keeps the operator from overstating what graph search can establish.

